 ],
 "literal": "Mature buds of corkwood tree, resembling millet, round flowers / like showering on the dark water lily flower, / O lord of the coast of abundant waters where the cold wind blows! / Even when her mother, having become angry, beats her, crying out / 'Mother!' like an infant, / even if you do unpleasant things, or even if you sweetly show great love, / my friend is within your protection; / she has no savior to remove her distress.",
 "translation": "O lord of the coast of abundant waters, where the cold wind blows and scatters the round flowers of the corkwood tree—resembling millet and looking like a shower of buds upon the dark water lilies! Like an infant who, even when its mother beats it in anger, cries out 'Mother!', my friend remains solely within your protection, whether you cause her grief or show her sweet love. She has no other savior to remove her distress.",
 "modern": "குளிர்ந்த காற்று வீசி, தினை போன்ற திரண்ட ஞாழல் மலர்களைக் கரிய நெய்தல் மலர்களின் மேல் தூவும் வலிமையான நீர்ப்பரப்பைக் கொண்ட கடற்கரைத் தலைவனே! கோபமடைந்த தாய் தன்னை அடிக்கும்போதும் 'அம்மா!' என்று கதறும் குழந்தையைப் போல, நீ துன்பமே செய்தாலும் அல்லது இன்பம் தரும் அன்பைச் செய்தாலும் என் தோழி உன்னையே தஞ்சமெனக் கொண்டவள். அவளுக்குத் தன் துன்பத்தைப் போக்க வேறு யாரும் இல்லை.",
 "explanation": "The friend of the heroine is speaking to the hero, who has been delaying their marriage and causing distress. She uses a powerful simile to describe the heroine's absolute devotion: just as an infant runs back to its mother crying 'Mother!' even when the mother beats it in anger, the heroine looks only to the hero for refuge, regardless of whether he causes her pain or shows her love. This poem belongs to the Neytal (maritime) landscape, where the cold wind scattering the millet-like yellow corkwood flowers onto the dark water lilies mirrors how the hero's actions deeply affect the heroine's life. The key words are ஞாழல் (corkwood tree), நெய்தல் (water lily), குழவி (infant), and வரைப்பினள் (one within protection).",
